=== REASONING ===

Design choice: this slice is the textbook application of the N4
template. The N5 surface (per-charging-session diagnosis) is the
exact same shape as the N4 surface (per-drive coaching), differing
only in the data domain. Mirroring slice 0018 verbatim minimises
both reviewer cognitive load and ADR-015 invariant-bypass risk.

Key design decisions:

(1) BASELINE COEXISTENCE (ADR-015 §I3, P10): the existing
    deterministic flag badges (chargingAggregation.ts) MUST remain
    the canonical classification. The new
    `query_charging_aggregation` Go tool MIRRORS the frontend
    thresholds VERBATIM rather than introducing a server-side
    re-computation. The unit test
    TestQueryChargingAggregation_ThresholdsMatchFrontend pins
    every threshold constant to its frontend counterpart so a
    future drift fails CI rather than silently corrupting the
    diagnosis. The slice prompt's "without changing how flags are
    computed" mandate is honoured at the constant level.

(2) DATA-DOMAIN SCOPING: handler URL parameter is `sessionID` (the
    prompt's name); backend route is
    POST /api/v1/ai/charging/{sessionID}/diagnose. The user
    message synthesised by the handler omits vehicle_id — the
    LLM learns the vehicle from the tool reply
    (query_charge_session returns *models.ChargingSession with
    its VehicleID field). Including a user-controllable vehicle
    hint in the prompt would risk cross-tenant leak via prompt
    injection.

(3) READ-ONLY TOOLS: both new tools (`query_charge_session`,
    `query_charging_aggregation`) declare Mutates() = false. The
    dispatcher's deny-all confirm gate refuses anything mutating;
    this slice ships zero mutating tools and adds nothing to the
    charging ingestion or aggregation pipeline. Consistent with
    "no duplicate write paths".

(4) REDACTION POLICY: PolicyChargingDiagnosis allow-list is exactly
    [ClassVehicleName] per the slice prompt's explicit
    "Allowed classes: ClassVehicleName only; charging location
    names remain tagged by default" line. Distinct from
    PolicyDriveCoaching as a separate identifier so a future
    per-feature edit does not bleed across the two; today they
    share the same allow-list value.

(5) PROVIDER REGISTRY USE: handler resolves the provider via
    *provider.Registry.For("charging-diagnosis"). Per-request
    resolution honours mid-flight settings changes (model swap,
    mode flip) without restart. A resolve failure short-circuits
    BEFORE opening the SSE stream so the frontend gets a JSON 502
    instead of a half-opened stream.

(6) DECORATOR CHAIN: by going through the provider registry, the
    methodology's locked decorator order
    (redaction → rate limit → cost cap → audit → trace) is applied
    automatically — the handler does not bypass any of them.

(7) PROVIDER PORT INTERFACE (P1): handler imports only
    `provider.Registry` + `strategy.Strategy` + `tools.Registry` —
    no direct provider adapter import (anthropic / openai / ollama
    are not imported here), in accordance with P1.

(8) STRATEGY PROVIDED VIA INTERFACE: handler signature accepts
    `strategy.Strategy` (the port), not `*chargingdiagnosis.Strategy`
    (the concrete type). Constructor wiring in router.go injects the
    concrete strategy instance.

(9) STRATEGY ↔ TOOLS ALIGNMENT: the strategy's allowedTools list
    (charging_diagnosis goldens.yaml feature.tools) MUST match the
    Go tool registration. The pin test
    TestStrategy_Tools enforces this on the strategy side; the
    canned-replies in the goldens enforce it on the eval side. A
    future addition to the whitelist that forgets to register the
    matching Go tool will fail at boot (dispatcher refuses unknown
    tools) AND in the eval harness.

(10) REGISTRY METADATA COMPLETE: charging-diagnosis registry entry
     populates ALL 5 RouteSet fields. UITestIDs +
     `data-testid="ai-feature-charging-diagnosis-root"` on the HOC
     wrapper means the future final-gate Playwright crawl can
     prove the surface is absent in off mode by query selector.
     PushKinds=[]string{} and JobNames=[]string{} are explicit
     empty arrays (not nil) so the registry CoverageOK() helper
     emits them as "[]" in the TS mirror; nil would fail the
     coverage round-trip.

(11) I18N STRINGS IN EN.JSON ONLY: ar.json + he.json are
     placeholder files (10 bytes each); i18next falls back to
     en.json. Same precedent as slices 0011-0018.

(12) EVAL HARNESS INVOCATION: the slice prompt's
     `make ai-eval-fast --feature charging-diagnosis` is not the
     canonical invocation (Make does not forward `--feature` to
     recipes). The canonical command, established by slices
     0011-0018 and used here, is
     `go run ./cmd/ai-eval --feature charging-diagnosis`. Result:
     3 goldens, 3 pass, 0 fail.

(13) REVIEWED BUT NOT TAKEN: a SectionErrorBoundary wrapper around
     <AIChargingDiagnosis sessionId={id} />. The DriveDetailPage
     uses one; the ChargingDetailPage does NOT use any
     SectionErrorBoundary anywhere on the page (every section
     renders inside <FadeIn> directly). For consistency with the
     surrounding page style we render <AIChargingDiagnosis>
     directly inside <FadeIn>. The withAiFeature HOC's null
     return when AI is off makes the error-boundary case
     unreachable in off mode (no children to throw); when AI is
     on the inner section is a static GlassPanel + disabled
     button which has no IO to throw from.

